# PII Pre-Review Gate

Run this before handing a branch to Copilot review. Your job is to prevent new
private data from entering the committed change set. This skill does not
authorize pushes, history rewrites, force-pushes, merges to main, or
credential rotation.

## Never Include in Plaintext

- passwords, tokens, API keys, webhook secrets, cookies, private keys, recovery
  codes, embedded URL credentials, or credential-bearing generated files;
- personal email addresses, phone numbers, chat user/channel/server/role/client
  IDs, account IDs, mailbox addresses, or other durable identifiers;
- real internal hostnames/domains, private IP mappings, MAC addresses, CIDRs,
  VLAN/subnet layouts, VPN peer details, NAS/device names, and other private
  topology;
- private-system usernames or filesystem paths that identify a person or expose
  protected infrastructure.

Usually safe: public vendor endpoints, public project/documentation URLs,
package and container image names, SSH public keys, host-key fingerprints,
generic service names, and ports. Evaluate context instead of vaulting every
number or hostname mechanically.

## Pre-Review Workflow

1. Read the relevant repo instructions and domain skills before editing.
2. Identify the intended file set with `git status --short`, the worktree diff
   against the base (`git diff origin/main...HEAD`), and untracked files.
   Preserve unrelated work; on a shared task branch, never commit another
   card's in-flight files.
3. Search every changed file and its filename for:
   - `BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY`, `AKIA`, `ghp_`, `gho_`, `github_pat_`, `xox*`,
     bearer headers, cookies, and credentials embedded in URLs;
   - `password`, `token`, `secret`, `api_key`, `private_key`, `authorization`,
     and credential payload fields;
   - email/phone patterns, chat mentions, and 15-20 digit platform IDs;
   - internal domains/hostnames, RFC1918 addresses, MACs, CIDRs, and VLANs.
4. Inspect surrounding code for values that evade signatures: concatenated
   tokens, template interpolation, base64, generated environment files, command
   arguments, API bodies, registered results, documentation examples, agent
   definitions, skills, and changelog prose.
5. Move sensitive Ansible values into existing Vault-backed variables. If a new
   value is required, use the `ansible-vault-secrets` skill and
   `ansible-vault encrypt_string --vault-password-file ~/ansible_key`. Never
   print the plaintext afterward or invent a replacement credential.
6. Add `no_log: true` to tasks whose arguments/results contain decrypted data;
   add `diff: false` to secret-bearing file/template/copy tasks. Use `0600` for
   credential files and `0700` for their directories unless the application
   requires stricter documented handling.
7. Render sensitive templates only to temporary mode-`0600` files. Assert safe
   fields without printing the file, then delete it.
8. Confirm local credential/config files are ignored and untracked. Run the
   commands with the REAL literal path of each candidate file (never a
   placeholder token), e.g.:

   ```bash
   git check-ignore -v .env changelogs
   git ls-files -- .env changelogs
   ```

9. Re-run the focused secret/PII search after remediation. Scanner examples,
   public endpoints, Vault ciphertext, and public keys are not findings unless
   context makes them identifying or usable.
10. Run the relevant syntax checks, lint, tests, template renders, and
    `{{ hermes_git_helper_bin }} check` (run inside the task worktree).
11. Commit only your intended files through the managed helper
    (`{{ hermes_git_helper_bin }} commit` — subject on line 1 of stdin, one
    pathspec per line after it, delivered via a quoted heredoc). Compare
    `{{ hermes_git_helper_bin }} files` set-to-set with the handoff
    list, sweep every branch commit message for leaks
    (`git log --format=%B` over the branch's own commit range), and inspect
    `git status --short` so nothing you claim is left uncommitted.
12. Append the required ignored CAB change log, then hand off to Copilot with
    the branch, base, commit list, changed paths, checks run, and any
    unresolved uncertainty.

## Stop Conditions

Do not pass the work for review when:

- a suspected credential or PII match is unresolved;
- a sensitive value is merely renamed, encoded, redacted in `HEAD`, or moved to
  another tracked file instead of being Vault-backed;
- a task can expose decrypted data in Ansible output;
- the branch file set differs from the handoff list;
- required validation did not run or failed.

If you discover that sensitive data may already exist in a commit on the task
branch, stop and escalate to Jimmy and Copilot before handing off — the branch
stays local, so remediation (follow-up commit or, with approval, branch-local
history fix) is Jimmy's call; the
`public-repo-audit` workflow belongs to the Copilot profile.